// expect-error: T033
//
// T033 / CT018 — `str` `==` (and `!=`) with a `@SecretCT` operand is rejected.
//
// `str` equality compares CONTENT with an early-exit byte loop. Its trip count
// reveals the length of the common prefix between the two strings, and so does
// the fuel it consumes — which the runtime surfaces to the caller. Either is a
// timing oracle over secret data, so the comparison is rejected outright.
//
// The gate lives in the taint checker rather than anywhere downstream because
// taint runs on the typed AST, strictly before AIR lowering. By the time the
// byte loop exists as a construct, the taint labels are gone.
//
// It rejects rather than silently selecting a constant-time lowering, for three
// reasons: taint results are not carried into lowering, so there is no channel
// to select on; a branch-free compare still leaks `min(len_a, len_b)` through
// both trip count and fuel; and the CT intrinsics (`ct_eq` / `ct_select` /
// `ct_lt`) are integer-only, so there is nothing to build a constant-time `str`
// compare out of today. Promising one would be a lie told in the compiler's own
// voice.
//
// Integer `==` on `@SecretCT` stays legal — a single machine instruction with
// no data-dependent control flow and nothing to time.
//
// The MUTATION_SITE below is the operand type. Change both parameters from
// `str` to `i64` and the fixture compiles cleanly, proving this pins the
// string-content comparison specifically and not merely the presence of
// `@SecretCT` on an operand.
module main;

// MUTATION_SITE
fn cmp(a: str @SecretCT, b: str @SecretCT) -> bool @SecretCT {
    return a == b;
}
